<file path="openspec/changes/e12-admin-frontend/proposal.md">
## Why

E10 defines the protected administration APIs, but administrators still need a safe, accessible interface for provider operations, fetch history, database metrics, logs, and runtime settings. E12 completes the PRD administration workflows (US-004 through US-008) and the Backlog TI-120 through TI-125 without moving authorization or operational rules into the browser.

## What Changes

- Add an Admin-only provider dashboard with live status, cadence, cooldown, supported-mode, and manual-fetch controls.
- Add paginated fetch-history presentation with safe, readable terminal errors and logical-job status refresh.
- Add database summary and bounded structured-log views.
- Add an atomic runtime-settings form with validation, unsaved-change protection, and application-time feedback.
- Add explicit loading, empty, stale, forbidden, conflict, and failure states for every administration workflow.

## Capabilities

### New Capabilities

- `admin-frontend-provider-operations`: Admin routes for provider status, cooldown-aware manual fetches, and paginated fetch history.
- `admin-frontend-observability`: Admin presentation of grouped database counts and bounded structured logs.
- `admin-frontend-runtime-settings`: Safe editing of non-secret provider and logging settings with atomic-save feedback.

### Modified Capabilities

- None.

## Impact

- `frontend/src/routes/admin`: new provider, summary, logs, and settings routes guarded by the E11 Admin session policy.
- `frontend/src/features/admin` and shared UI: typed E10 clients, query/mutation state, safe text presentation, forms, and reusable status components.
- Frontend tests: component, navigation, mutation-race, conflict, stale-state, and accessibility coverage.
- Depends on E11 shell/auth behavior and an E10 dashboard/settings contract that supplies authoritative per-mode trigger state, default mode, and provider minimum intervals; E12 itself makes no database, provider-execution, or secret-management changes.
</file>

<file path="openspec/changes/e13-quality-security-performance/specs/ci-quality-gates/spec.md">
## Purpose

Defines clean-checkout, merge-blocking quality checks that reproduce locally and retain enough evidence to diagnose failures and approve releases.

## ADDED Requirements

### Requirement: Clean-checkout required checks
CI SHALL pin supported .NET and Node toolchains and run backend restore/build with warnings as errors, formatting verification, unit tests, real SQL/RabbitMQ integration tests, frontend reproducible install, formatting, lint, component/flow tests, production build, dependency audits, strict OpenSpec validation, and every Dockerfile present in E13 scope. Any failed command SHALL fail its required check.

#### Scenario: Required command fails
- **WHEN** any build, test, validation, audit, or scoped container command exits unsuccessfully
- **THEN** the CI workflow fails and downstream release readiness cannot pass

### Requirement: Isolated CI services and evidence
CI SHALL start healthy isolated SQL Server and RabbitMQ services using test-only credentials, unique namespaces, no live provider requests, and no persistent developer state. Caches SHALL be keyed by lockfiles, stale runs SHALL be cancellable, and test/security results plus useful failure diagnostics SHALL be retained without secrets.

#### Scenario: Clean clone runs CI
- **WHEN** the workflow starts from a clean checkout without a developer `.env` or local volumes
- **THEN** all required configuration comes from test-safe CI inputs and service-backed checks can run deterministically

### Requirement: Merge protection is documented and checked
Repository automation SHALL document which workflow checks are required by branch protection and provide a repeatable verification step for that external configuration. Workflow success alone SHALL NOT be presented as merge blocking when branch protection is absent.

#### Scenario: Required-check protection is missing
- **WHEN** release review finds that a mandatory workflow is not enforced on the protected branch
- **THEN** the CI quality gate remains incomplete until the external setting is corrected
</file>

<file path="openspec/changes/e13-quality-security-performance/specs/critical-rule-verification/spec.md">
## Purpose

Makes release-critical product rules traceable to named automated tests instead of relying on a percentage coverage target or informal confidence.

## ADDED Requirements

### Requirement: PRD-to-test traceability
The repository SHALL maintain a machine-checkable manifest mapping every critical FR-001 through FR-012 rule, provider cadence/age boundary, identity rule, counter definition, and retry decision to one or more named automated tests. Existing tests SHALL count when they directly prove the rule. CI SHALL fail when a required rule is unmapped or its named test cannot be found.

#### Scenario: Mapped test is removed
- **WHEN** a referenced test is renamed or deleted without updating evidence
- **THEN** traceability validation fails and identifies the affected rule ID

### Requirement: Boundary-focused rule tests
Critical-rule tests SHALL use injected time and explicit boundary values for normalization, identity/hashing, validation order, provider cooldowns, the 180-day false-positive rule, lifecycle counters, and error classification. Failures SHALL identify the violated rule without exposing raw secret-bearing provider content.

#### Scenario: False-positive age boundary
- **WHEN** one IOC is exactly 180 days old and another is older than 180 days at the fixed evaluation time
- **THEN** only the older IOC is marked false positive, and a later current observation clears that state
</file>

<file path="openspec/changes/e13-quality-security-performance/specs/deterministic-test-fixtures/spec.md">
## Purpose

Supplies sanitized, deterministic provider samples and reusable test builders so all verification runs without live services or secret data unless a real boundary is explicitly required.

## ADDED Requirements

### Requirement: Sanitized provider fixtures
The repository SHALL contain immutable ThreatFox recent/full and PhishTank fixtures using reserved indicators and non-secret credentials. Together they SHALL cover IPv4, IPv6, Domain, URL, ports, every mapped provider field, null/false/zero/empty fidelity, duplicate native and normalized identities, malformed records, and an invalid final record for rollback verification.

#### Scenario: Fixtures run offline
- **WHEN** provider parser tests consume the committed fixtures
- **THEN** every case runs without network access or real provider keys and produces its documented records or deterministic safe error

### Requirement: Deterministic builders and fakes
Reusable builders SHALL provide injected time, repeatable dataset generation, stable identity/pagination ties, and fake provider/HTTP outcomes for success, timeout, 5xx, 429, corrupt or truncated content, cancellation, duplicates, and invalid records. The same seed and clock SHALL produce logically identical inputs.

#### Scenario: Dataset is reproduced
- **WHEN** two runs use the same generator seed and fixed time
- **THEN** their records, identities, ordering ties, and expected outcomes are identical
</file>

<file path="openspec/changes/e13-quality-security-performance/specs/frontend-flow-verification/spec.md">
## Purpose

Verifies critical User and Admin browser workflows, accessibility states, and hostile-text handling through deterministic frontend boundaries suitable for CI.

## ADDED Requirements

### Requirement: Deterministic frontend API boundary
Frontend component and flow tests SHALL use validated fixture contracts through an injected client or request-interception boundary and SHALL NOT require live providers, SQL Server, or RabbitMQ. The harness SHALL support loading, success, empty, 400, 401, 403, 404, 409, unexpected failure, and delayed response states.

#### Scenario: Same fixture is repeated
- **WHEN** a flow test runs repeatedly with the same API fixture
- **THEN** it renders the same state and sends the same contract requests in CI

### Requirement: Critical User and Admin flows
Automated browser-capable tests SHALL cover login, deep-link resume, expiry/logout/cache clearing, role routing, search validation/not-found/grouped success, browse URL filters and history, detail source attribution and relations, Admin provider/manual-trigger/history/summary/log/settings flows, failed-save preservation, and duplicate-action prevention.

#### Scenario: User attempts an Admin workflow
- **WHEN** a User session navigates to an Admin route
- **THEN** Admin data is not requested or rendered and the documented forbidden behavior occurs

### Requirement: Accessibility and unsafe-text regression coverage
Critical flows SHALL include keyboard, focus, accessible-name, dialog, status-announcement, and basic automated accessibility checks. Provider, IOC, log, and error strings containing markup SHALL remain inert text.

#### Scenario: Hostile external text is displayed
- **WHEN** a fixture contains HTML or script-like text in an external field
- **THEN** no executable markup is created and the literal value remains inspectable
</file>

<file path="openspec/changes/e13-quality-security-performance/specs/performance-assurance/spec.md">
## Purpose

Provides reproducible workload definitions, measurable ingestion/query budgets, and explicit capacity decisions for release-scale operation.

## ADDED Requirements

### Requirement: Versioned representative workloads
Performance verification SHALL define deterministic small and full-feed profiles recording their dataset fingerprint, type/provider/tag/port distribution, duplicate/update ratio, relation fan-out, runtime and SQL versions, hardware/resources, configuration, and chunk size.

#### Scenario: Benchmark is reproduced
- **WHEN** an operator reruns a profile with its recorded environment and seed
- **THEN** the generated workload fingerprint and measurement dimensions match the published run

### Requirement: Ingestion and query measurements
Measurements SHALL include ingestion wall time and throughput, peak process memory, peak tracked entities, transaction-log growth, and exact/detail/browse/relations/summary latency plus SQL logical reads and representative plans. PhishTank streaming SHALL be verified at no more than 256 MiB peak process memory for at least twice the sampled feed size.

#### Scenario: Full-feed profile completes
- **WHEN** the full-feed benchmark runs
- **THEN** its machine-readable result contains every required ingestion, resource, query, and environment measurement

### Requirement: Approved budgets or explicit capacity limit
A checked-in budget manifest and machine-readable comparison result SHALL exist before release. Missing evidence or a regression beyond an approved budget SHALL fail the performance gate unless a documented capacity limit records the measured limit, impact, owner, and remediation milestone. Full profiles SHALL run as scheduled/release checks; stable small regressions SHALL run for pull requests.

#### Scenario: Budget is exceeded
- **WHEN** a required metric exceeds its approved threshold
- **THEN** release fails or is explicitly marked capacity-limited with owned remediation rather than silently passing
</file>

<file path="openspec/changes/e13-quality-security-performance/specs/rabbitmq-system-verification/spec.md">
## Purpose

Proves message topology and worker lifecycle behavior against an isolated real RabbitMQ boundary with deterministic timing and identity.

## ADDED Requirements

### Requirement: Isolated real-broker harness
Broker verification SHALL use a real RabbitMQ instance with a unique virtual host or endpoint namespace per run. It SHALL prove durable topology, confirmed publishing, reconnect behavior, one-at-a-time consumption, redelivery idempotency, one logical history per `jobId`, immediate terminal failures, exhausted retry routing, and cleanup isolation.

#### Scenario: Duplicate delivery reuses one logical job
- **WHEN** the same `jobId` is delivered more than once
- **THEN** persisted data and history are applied no more than once and no second logical history is created

#### Scenario: Retries are exhausted
- **WHEN** a retryable job fails every allowed execution
- **THEN** executions follow the configured attempt order and the final message reaches the expected `_error` queue

### Requirement: Production retry contract remains verifiable
The production retry delays SHALL remain 10 seconds, 30 seconds, and 60 seconds with four total executions. Broker tests MAY inject shorter delays for deterministic CI, but a separate configuration test SHALL lock the production values and the real-broker test SHALL still verify attempt count, ordering, and terminal routing.

#### Scenario: CI uses shortened delays
- **WHEN** the integration harness replaces delays with bounded test values
- **THEN** the broker behavior is exercised quickly while a passing contract test independently proves the production schedule
</file>

<file path="openspec/changes/e13-quality-security-performance/specs/security-assurance/spec.md">
## Purpose

Establishes repeatable security review evidence and a fail-closed release rule for high-impact findings, dependencies, containers, and secret boundaries.

## ADDED Requirements

### Requirement: Versioned threat model and review
Each release candidate SHALL have a versioned review covering authentication and password timing, JWT/authorization, provider URL and redirect SSRF, archive/decompression bounds, stored external text, secrets/configuration, log/error redaction, `.env` read/write prohibition, CORS, dependencies, and container exposure. Every area SHALL record assets, trust boundaries, threats, controls, evidence, and residual risk.

#### Scenario: Review area lacks evidence
- **WHEN** a required threat area has no linked control or verification evidence
- **THEN** security review remains incomplete and release readiness fails

### Requirement: Finding closure policy
Findings SHALL use a documented severity rubric. Any open High or Critical finding SHALL block release. An accepted lower-severity residual risk SHALL record an owner, rationale, mitigation, and review date; fixed high-impact findings SHALL gain regression coverage and link their owning capability change when behavior changed.

#### Scenario: High finding remains open
- **WHEN** release readiness evaluates an unresolved High finding
- **THEN** the security gate fails regardless of other passing checks

### Requirement: Automated security gates
CI SHALL audit .NET and npm dependencies and scan relevant built images and repository artifacts for High/Critical vulnerabilities and known canary secrets. Logs and reports SHALL not print secret values.

#### Scenario: Known secret enters a tracked artifact
- **WHEN** a canary credential appears in source, history under review, or a built image
- **THEN** the security gate fails and identifies the artifact without echoing the full value
</file>

<file path="openspec/changes/e13-quality-security-performance/specs/sqlserver-system-verification/spec.md">
## Purpose

Proves schema, persistence, ingestion, grouped-query, and authenticated HTTP behavior against an isolated supported SQL Server rather than an in-memory substitute.

## ADDED Requirements

### Requirement: Isolated real-SQL harness
SQL verification SHALL use a supported SQL Server with a unique database per run or worker and reliable cleanup. Parallel jobs SHALL NOT reset or share another job's database, and EF InMemory SHALL NOT be used as evidence for SQL behavior.

#### Scenario: SQL suites run in parallel
- **WHEN** two CI workers execute integration suites concurrently
- **THEN** each uses a distinct database and neither deletes or changes the other's data

### Requirement: Schema, migration, and seed verification
Tests SHALL prove empty installation, ordered migration application, idempotency at the latest schema, and upgrade from each supported predecessor once more than one migration exists. Every documented constraint and index SHALL be checked; rejected writes SHALL assert the expected SQL error number and named constraint or index when SQL Server exposes it. Concurrent initialization SHALL converge without duplicate seeds.

#### Scenario: Duplicate identity violates the intended index
- **WHEN** a write duplicates a documented provider identity
- **THEN** SQL Server rejects it through the expected unique index rather than an unrelated failure

#### Scenario: Concurrent initialization
- **WHEN** two initializers target an empty or upgradeable database
- **THEN** migration and seed work converges on the latest schema with exactly one row per deterministic seed

### Requirement: Transaction, upsert, and query verification
Real-SQL tests SHALL prove whole-batch rollback, provider identity conflicts, repeat and concurrent upserts, exact counters and timestamps, tag replacement, source attribution, grouped exact/detail/browse/relations/summary behavior, aggregate null/tie ordering, and stable bounded pagination. Representative query evidence SHALL confirm intended indexes are usable.

#### Scenario: Invalid final record rolls back prior chunks
- **WHEN** the last record is invalid after earlier chunks were processed
- **THEN** no IOC/tag writes remain and the logical history is Failed with zero added and updated counters

#### Scenario: Tied pages remain stable
- **WHEN** repeated page requests operate on groups with tied aggregate sort values
- **THEN** pages return deterministic disjoint identities without skips or duplicates

### Requirement: Authenticated API verification on SQL persistence
HTTP integration tests SHALL cover admin seeding/login, JWT and User/Admin authorization, every E09 query endpoint, and every E10 Admin endpoint using real SQL persistence. Responses and persistence effects SHALL match their public contracts.

#### Scenario: Authorization and persistence matrix
- **WHEN** anonymous, User, and Admin clients exercise public and Admin endpoints
- **THEN** 401, 403, and successful outcomes match policy and successful operations read or change the expected SQL state
</file>

